# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# What must pass before main takes a merge. github-guard reads this from the
# default branch on the server and applies it to branch protection.
#
#   ci-ok   ci.yml: the one always-run gate. It fails when any job it needs
#           failed, was cancelled or was skipped: the unit tier with no
#           fixtures, the kernel-made fixtures built in the
#           fs-linux-test-harness VM, the whole suite (lint, unit, the
#           btrfs-progs oracles, the kernel oracles) on x86_64, the
#           VM-free tiers on aarch64, and the suite compiled and run
#           inside the guest.
#
# WHY A GATE RATHER THAN A LIST OF JOBS (#187). This file used to name two
# jobs — `test-ubuntu-latest` and `validate against kernel btrfs driver` —
# and that is a shape with two failure modes, both of which were live here.
# A renamed or split job leaves a required check that never reports, which
# blocks every pull request with nothing to point at; and an ADDED job is
# required by nobody, so a leg can run, go red, and merge anyway. The first
# came due immediately: the move into the harness VM renames both of those
# jobs out of existence and adds three more. Requiring the aggregate
# instead means jobs can be added, renamed or split freely, and the
# aggregate fails on a SKIPPED job too — which is the case a list of names
# cannot express at all.
#
# release.yml is deliberately absent: it triggers on a version tag, never on
# a pull request, and a required check that never reports blocks every merge
# forever.
#
# ONE NAME, AND IT IS THE AGGREGATE. `ci-ok` needs every other job in
# ci.yml and fails unless each concluded success, so a job renamed, split
# into a matrix leg or added is gated the moment it is in the workflow --
# without anyone editing this file. Naming the jobs here instead is what
# left new work required by nobody in five repositories (#197), and
# tests/ci_aggregate_gate.rs is what keeps the aggregate honest: it fails
# if a job is missing from ci-ok's needs, if ci-ok loses `if: always()`,
# or if this list names anything else (#187).
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
